WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.392 --> 00:00:02.100
POLONG LIN: In this
video, we'll show you

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.190
how you can predict user
churn for your gaming apps

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.090
using Google Analytics
4 and BigQuery ML.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.790
My name is Polong Lin, and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.513
for Google Cloud.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.930
MINHAZ KAZI: I'm Minhaz Kazi,
and I'm a developer advocate

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.070
for Google Analytics.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.860
If you're a mobile
game developer,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.070
a potential challenge for
you could be user retention.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.410
With so many games and
apps to choose from,

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.080
keeping users interested
in your product

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.680
and enabling them to come
back can be really difficult.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.030
According to a study in
2019, most mobile gamers

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:41.100
see only 25% retention
for users after 24 hours.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.730
That means on average, three
out of four users will churn.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.220
They'll first use their
app, and then after a day,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.540
they'll stop playing.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.990
So the question is, if
I'm mobile game developer,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.310
how do I reduce churn?

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:57.150
Polong, you have experience
with machine learning.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.950
Can that help here?

00:00:58.950 --> 00:00:59.910
POLONG LIN: Absolutely.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.130
At a high level, you
can use machine learning

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.050
to predict which of
your users are going

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.000
to churn based on
how they're behaving

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.850
in the app and their
demographic information.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.460
Once you have the predictions
of who's likely to turn,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.700
you can then target those users
who are perhaps on the fence

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.430
to incentivize them to come
back to the app or game,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.910
or make the game just a bit
more rewarding for them to play.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.310
Predicting each user's
propensity to churn,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.630
that's where machine
learning can help us.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.910
MINHAZ KAZI: User's
propensity to churn.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.830
That sounds interesting.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.750
Maybe we can apply this
to a real life example.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.870
We have a small little
game called Flood-It!

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.930
that's available both on the
App Store and Google Play Store.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.820
Can we identify
users' propensity

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.950
to churn for that app?

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:43.940
POLONG LIN: Certainly.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.190
As long as you have
the behavioral data,

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:47.870
you can build machine
learning models

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.470
to predict user propensity to
perform any specific behavior

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:54.680
like churn, or making
in-app purchases,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.330
or sharing the game with a
friend, just as examples.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.190
MINHAZ KAZI: So
here's a scenario.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.020
We have this
Flood-It! app and we

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.300
can use usage data from there.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.580
My goal is to identify
specific users who

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.240
are likely to churn and
then try to retain them.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.370
I can, for example,
through Firebase,

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.740
send notifications
to these users,

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:19.640
or customize the game to
be extra rewarding to them.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.130
Through Google Analytics,
I can segment the users

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.400
and analyze different
likelihoods of churn

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.270
and do marketing campaigns, or
through other marketing tools

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:33.840
tie together with email or
other marketing campaigns.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.767
So Polong, how am I going to
use machine learning to do this?

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:38.600
POLONG LIN: Hold on.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.670
There are a couple
of steps in order

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.412
to get to these
activation scenarios.

00:02:43.412 --> 00:02:44.870
Let's walk through
all the required

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.773
steps in reverse order.

00:02:46.773 --> 00:02:49.190
So you had just talked about
the last step, which is using

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.350
the predictions for activation.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:52.880
In order for that
to happen, you'll

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.220
need a churn prediction
model to give you

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.010
these predictions on
each user's likelihood,

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.645
or probability to churn.

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:01.520
And you can build this
machine learning model

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.738
using BigQuery ML.

00:03:02.738 --> 00:03:04.280
But to create this
model, you'll need

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.220
to first have prepared the
data in the right format

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.940
for the machine learning model.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.060
So we call this step
data preprocessing,

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.100
which is done in BigQuery.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.700
So that brings us
to the first step,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.660
which is making sure you have
your data ready in BigQuery.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.470
MINHAZ KAZI: Great.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.260
That fits in nicely since
we can use Google Analytics

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.500
4 to measure usage data
for the Flood-It! app.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.157
We can just export that
usage data into BigQuery.

00:03:28.157 --> 00:03:30.240
POLONG LIN: Minhaz, could
you tell us a little bit

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.370
about Google Analytics 4?

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.420
MINHAZ KAZI: If you're
not already familiar,

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.380
Google Analytics 4,
or GA4, is the newest

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.680
edition of Google Analytics.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.780
It allows you to
measure user engagement,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.460
not just for websites,
but also for apps.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.900
GA4 uses an event-based model.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.120
Usually, when the user or
the app does something,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.530
that's an event.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.900
If you're an app
developer who's already

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.240
been using Google
Analytics for Firebase,

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.740
you can be rest
assured that it's

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.860
the same data model as GA4.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.570
What's new with
Google Analytics 4

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:08.860
is that any user, free or
paid, can now export the GA4

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.590
data into BigQuery.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.340
POLONG LIN: And exporting
data to BigQuery

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.530
is really interesting,
because with BigQuery,

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.779
which is the data
warehouse on Google Cloud,

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:19.610
you can analyze terabytes
of data in seconds.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.820
And you can also train
and deploy machine

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:24.435
learning models, as you'll
learn in this video.

00:04:24.435 --> 00:04:25.810
So if you're a
marketing analyst,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.810
you can train and deploy
machine learning models

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.850
just by writing SQL without
any additional programming

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:32.140
languages.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.390
So Minhaz, how would a
Google Analytics user

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.430
export their data to BigQuery?

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.130
MINHAZ KAZI: I can log onto
the Google Analytics 4 property

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.650
and go to the Admin section.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.710
Then click on BigQuery Linking.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:48.700
On the right hand side, we will
see that, for this property,

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.220
no link has been
created to BigQuery.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.510
So we can click on link.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.745
Then it'll ask for which
BigQuery or Google Cloud

00:04:56.745 --> 00:04:59.240
Platform Project to link it to.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.562
If you haven't used BigQuery
before, at this point,

00:05:01.562 --> 00:05:03.770
you'll just need to set up
an account on Google Cloud

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.310
platform and select the
appropriate project.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.360
We'll select our project.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.150
And then click next.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:18.580
For our analysis, we'll
use the daily export.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.270
After you've reviewed and
submitted your expert options,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.930
GA4 will start exporting the
event data for this property

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.480
to the selected
BigQuery project.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.280
After a day or two, you should
start seeing the daily event

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.540
data in the events table.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.500
In the BigQuery UI, we can
click on the Preview tab,

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:42.940
and it'll show us what the
event export data looks like.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.085
It shows you the unique
event that occurred

00:05:45.085 --> 00:05:48.360
at a specific time stamp.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.300
You also get the event name
and additional information

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.930
about the event and
event parameters.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.120
For example, in
the first row, it

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:02.140
looks like the event was a level
complete quickplay by the user.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:05.370
So now that we have our GA
data in BigQuery, Polong,

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.260
what's our next step?

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.010
POLONG LIN: Well, in
order for the machine

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.230
learning algorithm to
train the model properly,

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.640
we need to pre-process
the raw GA4

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:14.370
data to be in the right format.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.190
This is an example of what
an ideal format might look

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.110
like for the training data.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.000
Specifically, every
row need to be

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.050
represented by a single user
along with a label of churned 1

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.430
or 0.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:29.460
We also want to include features
that can help the model learn,

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.280
specifically the user's
demographic information,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.640
and any sort of behavioral data
such as the number of times

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.770
they open the app within
a certain time period.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:42.150
So the way this works in
this data preprocessing stage

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.760
is, first, we'll need
to identify users

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.930
and the churned label,
process demographic features,

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.330
and process behavioral features.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:55.110
Then, we'll combine them
together as the training data.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.820
With the training data
ready, we can then

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.830
move on to building the
churn prediction model.

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:01.710
So the first step is
making sure we have

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.930
a solid definition of churn.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.540
Now, your definition
of churn may vary here.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.290
You may want to predict churn
after one day, one week,

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.020
or simply whether or not they
will churn up to the first 30

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.565
minutes of installing the app.

00:07:14.565 --> 00:07:16.800
And that definition
is entirely up to you

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.380
to decide what makes
sense for your app.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.950
For us, we chose to define
churn as the following.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:26.070
After 24 hours of a user's
first engagement with the app,

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:29.820
the users who never came
back we called churned.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.520
Those who come back after
24 hours, we can say they

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:33.700
returned.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.160
That means, given
all the user data

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.220
that we have, we want to say,
based on the user information

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.340
within the first 24 hours
of them using the app,

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.090
can we predict if they
will churn or return?

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:47.700
MINHAZ KAZI: But hold on,
what about those users who

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.460
use the app and then
within just a few minutes

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.590
they decide that it's not
the app for them at all?

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:56.070
Perhaps those users
aren't worth targeting

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.610
with any sort of incentives,
because this wasn't something

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.120
they were looking for.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.237
I would prefer to exclude them.

00:08:03.237 --> 00:08:04.570
POLONG LIN: That's a good point.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.230
Let's ignore users who left
the app within the first 10

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:08.100
minutes.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.557
We'll call these balanced users.

00:08:10.557 --> 00:08:12.390
So when we build the
machine learning model,

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.520
we'll only look at
users who didn't bounce.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.020
MINHAZ KAZI: So the
prediction model

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.540
will be based only on
the users who spend

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.000
at least 10 minutes on the app.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.650
To prepare the user
data in BigQuery,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.800
first we'll create a
bounced label column.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.890
Engagement is an event when the
user interacts with the app.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.990
If the user's last
engagement was

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:33.870
within 10 minutes of
their first engagement,

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:37.510
we'll consider that
the users have bounced.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:39.640
Bounced users will
get labeled with the 1

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.620
in the bounced column.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:45.190
Otherwise, the value is
0 for all and most users.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:48.670
Next, we'll create a
label column for churned.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:50.650
So if the user's
last engagement was

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.680
within 24 hours of
their first engagement,

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.230
we will consider the
user to have churned.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:59.440
Otherwise, we know that they
did something with the app

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.600
after the first
24 hours, meaning

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:02.900
that they did not churn.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:06.310
So we give those users a
value of churn equals 0.

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:08.260
So this is what
it looks like now.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:11.897
One label for churn and
one label for bounced.

00:09:11.897 --> 00:09:12.730
POLONG LIN: Perfect.

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.830
So in the next section,
we want to process

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:17.980
the demographic information
available for each user.

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.230
Minhaz, can you explain
what demographic data

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.480
is available in the GA4 data?

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.700
MINHAZ KAZI: In
GA events export,

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:26.530
some demographic
data about the user

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:28.990
is recorded automatically,
like the device

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:32.140
language and operating
system, user's browser,

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:34.000
and geographic information.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.330
You can also use user
parameters in GA4 to capture

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:40.130
additional user
demographic data.

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:44.210
For simplicity, we only chose
country, operating system,

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:46.670
and the user's device language.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.400
POLONG LIN: And here, if there
are multiple rows per user,

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:51.600
like if they were in
different countries,

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.020
then you can just use
perhaps the most recent

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:59.510
demographic information to
have only one row per user.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.150
So the next step is preparing
the behavioral data.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:03.705
And this step is
really important

00:10:03.705 --> 00:10:06.080
because you want to help the
machine learning model learn

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.350
how the user's behavior
in those first 24 hours

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:11.540
predict whether
or not they churn.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.910
Minhaz, what sort of behavioral
data does GA4 capture,

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.290
and what fields will
you consider using?

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:21.410
MINHAZ KAZI: As we mentioned
before, each row in GA4 data

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:22.580
is an event.

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:26.120
Some events are automatically
captured by GA4.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:29.840
There are recorded events for
specific use cases like gaming.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:34.220
You can also create custom
events, depending on your app.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:37.630
Let's see what type of events
we have in the flooded data set.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:40.660
We'll query all the event
names and sort by the account

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.430
across the data set.

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:44.820
As you can see, there
are automatically

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.510
collected events
like screen_view

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:48.730
and user_engagement.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.280
The other events are
recorded events for gaming,

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.040
like level_start_quickplay
and post_score.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:58.520
From the list, we chose
this subset of events

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.970
to be most important
for our analysis.

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.070
But you can choose
whichever events

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:07.940
you think might help the
ML model determine who is

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:10.637
likely to churn for your app.

00:11:10.637 --> 00:11:12.470
POLONG LIN: So we'd
like this sort of result

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:16.730
where each row is a unique
user ID, and for each user

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:20.300
we have some behavioral stats,
like how many times they engage

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:22.250
with the app, how
many times they

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:25.760
started a level with quickplay
or ended a level with quickplay

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.748
and so on.

00:11:27.748 --> 00:11:30.290
MINHAZ KAZI: We can achieve this
by counting different events

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:32.660
in different columns
for each user.

00:11:34.832 --> 00:11:36.290
POLONG LIN: Now
that we've prepared

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:39.680
our label, the demographic
data, and behavioral data,

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:41.810
we can combine them into
a single table, which

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.882
becomes our training data.

00:11:44.882 --> 00:11:46.840
MINHAZ KAZI: A quick side
note before moving on

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:48.190
to machine learning.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.910
Let's assume that you have other
demographic or behavioral data

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:55.540
from outside of GA
that you want to use.

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:59.200
For example, the data
could be in your CRM.

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:01.750
You can import that
data into BigQuery

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:04.570
and then join them
using the user ID,

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:07.360
and use that for
training as well.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.540
POLONG LIN: So with the
training data ready, now

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.088
we can finally
talk about building

00:12:11.088 --> 00:12:12.630
the machine learning
model to predict

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.840
churn using BigQuery ML.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:18.120
In this section, we'll
be using BigQuery ML

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:21.990
to train the churn prediction
model, evaluate the model,

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:23.970
and make predictions
on new users

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:26.850
to get their
probability of churning.

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.280
If you're not familiar
with what BigQuery ML is,

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.130
it's the ML, or machine
learning functionality,

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:34.420
that's built into
BigQuery by default.

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.200
So if your data is stored
in BigQuery, without needing

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:40.650
to move it out, you can use
BigQuery ML to train and deploy

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.380
machine learning models
directly using SQL.

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:46.470
You don't need to know
Python, R, or TensorFlow.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.930
You also don't need to set
up any extra infrastructure

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.660
because it uses BigQuery's
auto-scaling infrastructure

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.427
to train and deploy
your ML models.

00:12:54.427 --> 00:12:56.760
So that means you get your
data storage, data analytics,

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.227
and machine learning
all built into BigQuery.

00:12:59.227 --> 00:13:01.560
There are lots of different
machine learning model types

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.750
available with BigQuery ML.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:05.460
For propensity
modeling, you'll want

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.183
to use one of these supported
classification models.

00:13:08.183 --> 00:13:09.600
To keep things
simple, we'll stick

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:11.460
with logistic
regression in this video

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.980
as it's typically
the fastest to train.

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:16.230
You can always try to improve
your models afterwards,

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.570
but this gets you to a
working model quickly.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:21.480
So to train a logistic
regression model using SQL,

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.640
first, you'll need
your training data.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.610
Select star from the table
you just put together.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.230
Next, you'll want to create
a replace model followed

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:33.360
by the project and data set and
the name of the model, which

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.850
we've chosen here
to be churn_logreg.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.530
Next, we'll need
to specify that we

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:40.170
want to use logistic
regression as well as setting

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.990
the churned column to be
the target variable that we

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:44.430
want to predict.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:45.790
And that's all you need.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:47.340
And once the model
is trained, you

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.830
can evaluate it in BigQuery ML.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.390
And to do so, you can
use the BigQuery UI,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.630
which shows you the
evaluation metrics

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.490
when you select the
model you've trained.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:14:00.030
You can see the metrics
like log loss, ROC AUC,

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:04.740
precision, recall, accuracy, F1
score, and some visualizations.

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:07.620
You can also evaluate the
model using select star

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:11.003
from ML.EVALUATE and
then the model name.

00:14:11.003 --> 00:14:12.420
If you create
multiple models, you

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:15.450
can use something like
ROC AUC or F1 score

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:18.210
to compare your models, with
better models having scores

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.400
closer to 1.0.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:21.990
And depending on
your needs, this

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.720
is where you could be iterating
between training the model

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:27.630
and evaluating the model to
try to improve your model

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:28.980
performance.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:31.140
There are various approaches
to improving models.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.280
You can try a
different model type,

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:36.420
such as boosted tree classifier,
deep neural networks,

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.790
or even auto ML
tables, all which

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.560
are supported in BigQuery ML.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:43.290
You can also try to select
different features, or more

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:47.665
features, or engineer new
features out of existing ones.

00:14:47.665 --> 00:14:49.290
And, of course, you
can also try tuning

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.930
some of the hyperparameters
of your models.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:55.900
To learn more, check
out the documentation.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:56.890
MINHAZ KAZI: Great.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:59.200
So now with the
model created, how

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:03.622
do we make predictions on who
might actually churn or return?

00:15:03.622 --> 00:15:05.080
POLONG LIN: You
can use select star

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:08.920
from ML.PREDICT followed by
the model and the data you

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.680
want to make predictions on.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:12.520
Note that, for the
test data, it should

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:15.940
have the same columns as you
had in the training data.

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:19.000
We can see what the model
predicted in the first column,

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.655
but this isn't actually
what we are looking for.

00:15:21.655 --> 00:15:23.500
For propensity
modeling, we actually

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:27.070
want a probability of how
likely someone is to churn,

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:29.950
some value between 0 and 1.

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:32.560
So for this, we'll need to
extract out the probability

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:36.880
from the third column called
predicted_churned_probs.prob

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:40.760
for when the label
is equal to 1.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:43.340
To do so, you can extract
the probability of churn

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:47.500
for each user using
this SQL query.

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:50.710
Now that we have the
user's propensity to churn,

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.260
how do you use these
predictions for activation?

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:54.550
Minhaz?

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.670
MINHAZ KAZI: To get the
data into an usable state,

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:00.970
we can either-- we did
directly from BigQuery,

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:06.700
or export it into Google Cloud
Storage, or GCS, as CSV files.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:09.970
This query will export
the selected columns

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:13.560
into a CSV file in a GCS bucket.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:17.580
We can use either the BigQuery
data, or the exported CSV file,

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:22.050
in different ways depending
on how the [INAUDIBLE] works.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:25.440
For example, Firebase can
directly import from BigQuery,

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:30.910
whereas external marketing tools
might need to use CSV files.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:33.970
In GA4, you can
use the data import

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:38.630
feature to import the predicted
values as a user property.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:40.510
And then the user
property can be

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:43.960
used to create segments
and remarketing lists.

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:46.520
If you're using
Firebase for your app,

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.990
you can also import the
predictions back into Firebase

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:53.170
to tailor the user experience
using Remote Config,

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:56.860
or send notifications
to the user using Cloud

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:01.120
Messaging and In-App Messaging.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.370
You might also use
other external tools

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:05.950
to run marketing campaigns,
like sending out emails

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:08.400
to motivate users to return.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:10.750
Using the steps
described in this video,

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:12.450
we should be able to
do the activations

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.210
and finally increase the
number of users retained

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:19.109
for the Flood-It! app and reduce
the number of users churned.

00:17:19.109 --> 00:17:19.890
POLONG LIN: Great.

